course: dictionary
lesson: 13488
title: Ignite: starting fires and strong feelings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To ignite means to start something burning, or to make something start burning. «The spark ignited the dry grass.» The spark is the cause, and the grass catches fire.
explain: The word can also mean to make a feeling suddenly strong. «Her speech ignited hope in the crowd.» Nobody lit a real fire here. Hope suddenly grew strong inside the people listening.
explain: Context clues help you work out the meaning. Look for fire words like spark, flames or burn, or feeling words like anger and hope. Then swap in your meaning: «The spark started the grass burning» still makes sense, so ignite fits.
fact: Ignite means to start burning, or to make something start burning.
fact: Fuel ignites when it is hot enough and has oxygen.
fact: The fire triangle is fuel, heat and oxygen. Take one away and the fire cannot keep burning.
fact: Keep flammable things away from flames, because they can ignite easily.
fact: Matches and lighters are for adults to use.
exercise: «The spark ignited the dry leaves.» What does ignited mean? | Fell softly to the ground | Turned cold and wet | Blew away in the wind | *Started burning
exercise: «Her speech ignited hope in everyone.» What does ignited mean here? | *Made the hope suddenly strong | Put the hope out | Wrote the hope down on paper | Lost all its hope
exercise: Which thing should be kept well away from flames because it can ignite easily? | A metal spoon | A smooth stone | *Dry paper | A glass of water
test: «Dry wood ignites easily when it gets hot enough.» Which word means catches fire? | *Ignites | Cools | Drips | Shrinks
test: A fire needs fuel, and it also needs two other things. Which two? | *Heat and oxygen | Sugar and salt | Ice and wind | Water and sand
exam: «The news ignited anger across the town.» What does ignited suggest? | The news was forgotten by everyone | The town became quiet and calm | *Anger suddenly grew strong across the town | The anger was put out
